<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-05.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="012956"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#012956" label="[01:29:56]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: I think it is best if the tcc-linux-musl.iso bootstrap built upon the Fiwix kernel step which seems the logical stopping point for live-bootstrap for your purposes and improvements to fiwix would benefit all bootstraps.</span></div><div class="line" id="013118"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013118" label="[01:31:18]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: it has about 2TB/month bandwidth cap but can burst up to 100MB/s bandwidth as it is hosted in Linode (if that is acceptable to you and your needs)</span></div><div class="line" id="013422"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013422" label="[01:34:22]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: I think the eras of live-bootstrap would probably best be broken at: 1) after mescc-tools-extra 2) after fiwix and 3) after modern linux toolchain and distros probably would best be served using 2/3</span></div><div class="line" id="013615"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013615" label="[01:36:15]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mid-kid: we can always mirror sources in <a rel="nofollow" href="https://files.bootstrapping.world/">https://files.bootstrapping.world/</a>  and on the archive.org if you worry about them being future available. (I also have a public git repo hosting a copy of them too)</span></div><div class="line" id="013802"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013802" label="[01:38:02]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.sr.ht/~oriansj/sources/tree">https://git.sr.ht/~oriansj/sources/tree</a>  (anyone can send a pull request and I'll merge and mirror on the server)</span></div><div class="line" id="014035"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014035" label="[01:40:35]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: It was designed to provide the minimal core of haskell if one wanted to use haskell to improve the bootstrap chain at the M2-Planet level. (other better haskells exist that could be bootstrappped by later compilers) but ultimately no one used it and it was just another blind path of mine.</span></div><div class="line" id="014231"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014231" label="[01:42:31]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GHC remains a major bootstrapping problem.</span></div><div class="line" id="014314"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014314" label="[01:43:14]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, as a guix user, I cannot build hugs because gcc 4.9 fails to build, meanwhile nhc98 is written in haskell, but built from generated C files</span></div><div class="line" id="014521"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014521" label="[01:45:21]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as a guix user I get hash mismatches on the distfiles for gnutls, libgcrypt, libgpg-error, and openssl-1.1.1l won't pass tests because all the certs in the test suite have expired</span></div><div class="line" id="015303"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015303" label="[01:53:03]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't mind programming in mini-version of haskell that is easy to bootstrap, all I want are lazy evaluation and strong typechecking, it's just this mini-version, sadly, won't be able to build existing projects like darcs</span></div><div class="line" id="021011"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#021011" label="[02:10:11]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: Nice! I'll try to collect a list of them next time around.</span></div><div class="line" id="023056"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#023056" label="[02:30:56]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION wonders if there has been discussion about bootstrapping VCSen before, for eg downloading the VCS source for pijul requires pijul <a rel="nofollow" href="https://pijul.org/downloads">https://pijul.org/downloads</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="023626"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023626" label="[02:36:26]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">wait, what?</span></div><div class="line" id="023634"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023634" label="[02:36:34]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">how do distros package pijul then?</span></div><div class="line" id="023830"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023830" label="[02:38:30]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: same way they package a bunch of *shit*; they just take a binary blob and distribute that to their users</span></div><div class="line" id="023845"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023845" label="[02:38:45]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even guix does that for Haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="023952"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023952" label="[02:39:52]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in case with freebsd I see pijul-1.0.0-beta.9.crate</span></div><div class="line" id="023953"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023953" label="[02:39:53]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we just have the possibility if the bootstrap chains are preserved; for us to figure out a route to complete their chains later and thus solve it.</span></div><div class="line" id="024105"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#024105" label="[02:41:05]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess &quot;*.crate&quot; is some sort of archive format, I'm completely disconnected from rust</span></div><div class="line" id="024356"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024356" label="[02:43:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lol, that's just obtuse</span></div><div class="line" id="024422"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024422" label="[02:44:22]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">only distributing your source code via the thing i am trying to build is absolutely stupid</span></div><div class="line" id="024428"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024428" label="[02:44:28]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">does anyone actually package this thing?</span></div><div class="line" id="024444"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024444" label="[02:44:44]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">.crate is just a tar.gz</span></div><div class="line" id="024454"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024454" label="[02:44:54]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">of rust code for a library</span></div><div class="line" id="024458"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024458" label="[02:44:58]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with some metadata about it</span></div><div class="line" id="024517"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#024517" label="[02:45:17]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not a binary</span></div><div class="line" id="024601"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#024601" label="[02:46:01]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">^ this is true, that is how distributions do it, but i would really prefer if they provided an official .tar.gz of their source code</span></div><div class="line" id="025611"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025611" label="[02:56:11]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">imagine if all code written in rust was available only through pijul</span></div><div class="line" id="025703"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025703" label="[02:57:03]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">still, freebsd just downloads .crate for everything</span></div><div class="line" id="030020"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#030020" label="[03:00:20]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also it's &quot;awesome&quot; that every language has its own package manager that mimics what system distros are already doing</span></div><div class="line" id="030538"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030538" label="[03:05:38]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: well there is a very long tradition of each new cycle to recreate the mistakes of the last cycle. I just hope Guix and Nix packaging provide a long term path out.</span></div><div class="line" id="031213"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#031213" label="[03:12:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">eh, not sure it's fair to say that it mimics what system distros are doing, because language package managers often support different versions, where as your typical distro has one version per package</span></div><div class="line" id="031318"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031318" label="[03:13:18]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in guix there are many packages with many versions</span></div><div class="line" id="031731"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031731" label="[03:17:31]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">still, in distros the preferred practice is that all packages depend on the same version of specific library, at the very least it makes the whole build faster, because only one version of library is built</span></div><div class="line" id="031818"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031818" label="[03:18:18]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also I don't recall lts stackage mixing different versions of same libraries</span></div><div class="line" id="032422"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032422" label="[03:24:22]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Installing multiple versions of the same library side by side isn't a problem in most distros</span></div><div class="line" id="032429"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032429" label="[03:24:29]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the problem is that the library needs to support this</span></div><div class="line" id="032445"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032445" label="[03:24:45]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">usually by having a different filename for the different version</span></div><div class="line" id="032501"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032501" label="[03:25:01]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if the whole language ecosystem supports this, it's not really a problem at all.</span></div><div class="line" id="032521"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#032521" label="[03:25:21]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">don't high-level languages just link everything statically?</span></div><div class="line" id="032544"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032544" label="[03:25:44]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's less a high-level language thing and more a &quot;modern&quot; thing</span></div><div class="line" id="032556"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032556" label="[03:25:56]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">where people prefer ease of distribution over disk space and compile times</span></div><div class="line" id="032615"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032615" label="[03:26:15]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">rust links everything statically as does go</span></div><div class="line" id="032643"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032643" label="[03:26:43]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this doesn't mean however that a system package manager can't manage static libraries, precompiled or not</span></div><div class="line" id="032701"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#032701" label="[03:27:01]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, can you really have dynamic linking in languages with static polymorphism?</span></div><div class="line" id="032731"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032731" label="[03:27:31]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">debian packages all rust crates it uses and just installs them as source code to be used when building rust packages</span></div><div class="line" id="032804"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032804" label="[03:28:04]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://faultlore.com/blah/swift-abi/">https://faultlore.com/blah/swift-abi/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="032811"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032811" label="[03:28:11]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">swift did it</span></div><div class="line" id="032827"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032827" label="[03:28:27]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and so does C++</span></div><div class="line" id="032829"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032829" label="[03:28:29]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">honestly</span></div><div class="line" id="033036"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#033036" label="[03:30:36]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the reason swift did it is because apple manages an entire system and values integration and easily being able to upgrade things that even proprietary apps use</span></div><div class="line" id="033050"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#033050" label="[03:30:50]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whereas rust is only concerned with delivering applications</span></div><div class="line" id="033215"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#033215" label="[03:32:15]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION can't find word &quot;dispatch&quot; in that page</span></div><div class="line" id="033252"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#033252" label="[03:32:52]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I assume you mean dynamic dispatch as oppossed to static dispatch in polymorphism</span></div><div class="line" id="033529"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#033529" label="[03:35:29]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even though go links everything statically, interfaces in go are dynamically dispatched</span></div><div class="line" id="034247"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#034247" label="[03:42:47]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is there a specific language you're considering that does cross-library static dispatch?</span></div><div class="line" id="034503"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034503" label="[03:45:03]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, it can be any language with static polymorphism like haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="034752"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034752" label="[03:47:52]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the difference between static and dynamic is that in dymanic you can, for example, have a list of Eq: [32, &quot;string&quot;, Point {x: 1, y: 8}]</span></div><div class="line" id="034801"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034801" label="[03:48:01]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is how interfaces in go work</span></div><div class="line" id="034823"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034823" label="[03:48:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but in haskell static polymorphism will reject that</span></div><div class="line" id="040923"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040923" label="[04:09:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in a way it's implementation detail, you can have compiler for statically polymorphic language that does static dispatch and another compiler for the same language that does dynamic dispatch, the first produces faster code, which makes it comfortable for end-users, but second just compiles faster, which makes it comfortable for developers</span></div><div class="line" id="040927"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040927" label="[04:09:27]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://research.swtch.com/generic">https://research.swtch.com/generic</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="041000"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041000" label="[04:10:00]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">generics is static polymorphism</span></div><div class="line" id="042039"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#042039" label="[04:20:39]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mid-kid: that's all I needed to read <a rel="nofollow" href="https://faultlore.com/blah/swift-abi/#polymorphic-generics">https://faultlore.com/blah/swift-abi/#polymorphic-generics</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="042138"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#042138" label="[04:21:38]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">swift does dynamic dispatch</span></div><div class="line" id="050235"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050235" label="[05:02:35]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; homo: old GHC is easier to target because it's less source code - but even the earliest surviving version of GHC's source code is something like 20MiB already</span></div><div class="line" id="050256"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050256" label="[05:02:56]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Modern GHC's code base is so complicated that it's kind of a miracle that it can even build itself</span></div><div class="line" id="050319"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#050319" label="[05:03:19]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">kinda like web browsers and linux kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="050359"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050359" label="[05:03:59]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; At least those aren't self-hosting</span></div><div class="line" id="050415"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050415" label="[05:04:15]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; well, the kernel kind of is</span></div><div class="line" id="050427"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050427" label="[05:04:27]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; but you don't need to build all of the source code to get a working kernel capable of hosting its own build</span></div><div class="line" id="050450"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050450" label="[05:04:50]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; most of it is driver code that's never gonna be used on your system, whatever system you might have</span></div><div class="line" id="050528"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050528" label="[05:05:28]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; as opposed to GHC, where most of the code is for language features that are used in the very code that implements them</span></div><div class="line" id="050625"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050625" label="[05:06:25]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; contrast that with even GCC - supports C++20 and many other languages, but only needs a C++11 compiler to build</span></div><div class="line" id="050807"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#050807" label="[05:08:07]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you mean it's difficult to even rebuild libraries that ghc depends on?</span></div><div class="line" id="050848"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050848" label="[05:08:48]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Libraries are another can of worms; recent GHCs actually use built-in dependency resolution for their own code</span></div><div class="line" id="050906"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#050906" label="[05:09:06]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; But I'm just referring to the included Haskell source code that implements the language</span></div><div class="line" id="051010"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051010" label="[05:10:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; It's not written in a restricted or even well-defined subset of the language, but instead uses almost every feature</span></div><div class="line" id="051102"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#051102" label="[05:11:02]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also both C++11 and C++20 are standards and have competing implementations, but that's not case with haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="051141"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051141" label="[05:11:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; &amp; the modern GHC-Haskell dialect is so complex and often poorly documented that there's little chance of anyone writing a compatible reimplementation in a different language, or even in a more restricted Haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="051227"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#051227" label="[05:12:27]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">isn't it nightmare to maintain then?</span></div><div class="line" id="051238"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051238" label="[05:12:38]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; in fact, I would hazard a guess that for most of GHC's code as it stands today, not even its own authors could independently rewrite in even in full GHC-Haskell while retaining the ability to compile GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="051458"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051458" label="[05:14:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Another way to put it - it took ~30 years for GHC to be written; maybe it could be independently reimplemented in another 5-10 years of effort, but by then, GHC itself moves on to be even more complex</span></div><div class="line" id="051528"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#051528" label="[05:15:28]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and I thought rust was bad...</span></div><div class="line" id="051543"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051543" label="[05:15:43]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; With Rust, you have a specification to help you</span></div><div class="line" id="051608"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051608" label="[05:16:08]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; &amp; strict assurances to make sure rustc's own code doesn't depend on undocumented rustc-specific extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="051707"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051707" label="[05:17:07]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; By contrast, the oldest GHC we have (v0.26) is already written in an extended version of Haskell 1.2 that was only ever implemented in GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="051806"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051806" label="[05:18:06]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; v0.24 was the last version written in a standardized version of Haskell, and the last one to be ever successfully built by a compiler other than a prior GHC - and it's completely lost</span></div><div class="line" id="051836"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#051836" label="[05:18:36]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">are these features are used outside of ghc?</span></div><div class="line" id="051846"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051846" label="[05:18:46]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Some are, some aren't</span></div><div class="line" id="051927"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#051927" label="[05:19:27]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Most notably, the module system would later become part of the standard in Haskell 98 (IIRC)</span></div><div class="line" id="052131"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052131" label="[05:21:31]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; My hope is that 0.26 is still close enough to 0.24 that it's at least feasible to reverse engineer and reimplement the needed features in another 1.2-compliant compiler that's not written in Haskell - which is where Yale Haskell comes into play, as it's meant to be 1.2- and 1.3-compliant</span></div><div class="line" id="052204"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052204" label="[05:22:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; last version of Yale Haskell we have is from mid-1994, while GHC 0.26 was late 1995</span></div><div class="line" id="052320"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052320" label="[05:23:20]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a lot of old code bases are actually hard to build</span></div><div class="line" id="052343"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052343" label="[05:23:43]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so hard it might be easier to just create new compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="052423"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052423" label="[05:24:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://downloads.haskell.org/ghc/latest/docs/users_guide/exts/table.html">https://downloads.haskell.org/ghc/latest/docs/users_guide/exts/table.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="052435"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052435" label="[05:24:35]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; We know that 5.04 is new enough that Guix can build it using 4.08 (IIRC)</span></div><div class="line" id="052457"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052457" label="[05:24:57]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">another thing I thought was bad about C++ and rust is bloat in the language</span></div><div class="line" id="052523"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052523" label="[05:25:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">guix cannot build gcc 4.9 and therefore cannot build hugs, because it depends on gcc 4.9</span></div><div class="line" id="052547"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052547" label="[05:25:47]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; &amp; then on, it's possible to bootstrap up to 7.4 (up to 7.0 is implemented currently in Guix, but it's easy extend to 7.4)</span></div><div class="line" id="052632"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052632" label="[05:26:32]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; then in 7.6, hell breaks loose, so the next buildable version is 7.10, using a downloaded binary of 7.8</span></div><div class="line" id="052646"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052646" label="[05:26:46]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; and from then, up to 9.x</span></div><div class="line" id="052755"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052755" label="[05:27:55]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Hugs is unfortunately useless in and of itself, as it has no support for recursive module dependencies, which GHC's source code has always consistently had since 0.26</span></div><div class="line" id="052817"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052817" label="[05:28:17]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hugs is useful for personal projects</span></div><div class="line" id="052820"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#052820" label="[05:28:20]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; and AFAIK it's not simply unimplemented, but rather a fundamental limitation in Hugs' design</span></div><div class="line" id="052942"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052942" label="[05:29:42]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean when I program in haskell, I want lazy evaluation, IO monad, static type checking and syntax that is not painful to write</span></div><div class="line" id="053031"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053031" label="[05:30:31]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Writing a &quot;simple&quot; Haskell like blynn on microhs is the &quot;easy&quot; part (not that it's actually &quot;easy&quot; or &quot;simple&quot;, just in comparison) - the hard part is extending it to where it can compile GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="053059"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053059" label="[05:30:59]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Basically if a compiler can understand GHC, it can probably understand every Haskell program that exists</span></div><div class="line" id="053116"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053116" label="[05:31:16]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; That's not even true of Rust!</span></div><div class="line" id="053249"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#053249" label="[05:32:49]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I looked at clean, it looks like haskell without IO monad, but I don't know if it's bootstrapped</span></div><div class="line" id="053319"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#053319" label="[05:33:19]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm not aware of any other functional language that has lazy evaluation</span></div><div class="line" id="053356"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053356" label="[05:33:56]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Miranda and LazyML are the only other ones</span></div><div class="line" id="053401"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053401" label="[05:34:01]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; LazyML is, well, dead</span></div><div class="line" id="053415"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053415" label="[05:34:15]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; it's what was originally used to bootstrap GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="053458"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053458" label="[05:34:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; there are only 2 LazyML compilers known, one's written in itself, the other in _cough_ Haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="053534"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#053534" label="[05:35:34]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Miranda and Clean were both originally proprietary, the free versions were built by proprietary ones that came before them</span></div><div class="line" id="053934"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#053934" label="[05:39:34]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it must be really hard to implement declarative languages using imperative languages</span></div><div class="line" id="054008"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#054008" label="[05:40:08]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like in haskell it's not difficult to create languages using parsing combinators</span></div><div class="line" id="054114"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#054114" label="[05:41:14]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but I never tried creating even math expression evaluator in imperative languages</span></div><div class="line" id="062617"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#062617" label="[06:26:17]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It's really not as bad as you might think. Crafting Interpreters implements some pretty good evaluators in Java and C, which gets you good portable idioms for lexing and parsing</span></div><div class="line" id="062719"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#062719" label="[06:27:19]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I have actually been working on a runtime for lazy purely functional languages in -Wall-Wextra-pedantic-clean C99, but I discovered some mistakes forced me to restart and I haven't had a chance to get back to it for a few months</span></div><div class="line" id="081422"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#081422" label="[08:14:22]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">[04:15] &lt;matrix_bridge&gt; &lt;googulator&gt; With Rust, you have a specification to help you</span></div><div class="line" id="081424"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#081424" label="[08:14:24]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">[04:16] &lt;matrix_bridge&gt; &lt;googulator&gt; &amp; strict assurances to make sure rustc's own code doesn't depend on undocumented rustc-specific extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="081425"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#081425" label="[08:14:25]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">?</span></div><div class="line" id="103557"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#103557" label="[10:35:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: i am not at all concerned about fiwix within live-bootstrap and i will not touch any known-good working state</span></div><div class="line" id="103811"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#103811" label="[10:38:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently i am testing musl-1.1 and musl-1.2, and thinking about how to shrink the devutils.squashfs package-set down to something reasonable</span></div><div class="line" id="103851"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#103851" label="[10:38:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so far it seems both musl-1.1 and musl-1.2 are functional against a linux-2.4 ABI (patched for nptl support)</span></div><div class="line" id="104555"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#104555" label="[10:45:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">time64 stat/stat64 handling with musl-1.2 requires carefull review/testing still, but it's good to know musl-1.2 is prepared for year2038</span></div><div class="line" id="132320"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#132320" label="[13:23:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i have an idea, which simplifies publication, i'll publish build-scripts only</span></div><div class="line" id="132409"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#132409" label="[13:24:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's just i was told a complete distribution driven by tcc would not be feasible nor desireable</span></div><div class="line" id="132749"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#132749" label="[13:27:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i could upload the gentoo overlay and a bunch of shell scripts, but that's total chaos</span></div><div class="line" id="132758"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#132758" label="[13:27:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i wouldn't want to produce any more noise nor confusion</span></div><div class="line" id="133415"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#133415" label="[13:34:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nonetheless, it is feasible, and all that's remaining is another few iterations of cleanup and scripting</span></div><div class="line" id="184248"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#184248" label="[18:42:48]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">still, building modern ghc by chain-building all ghc versions starting from 0.26 is going to make it extremely hard to adjust source code to build on different OSes and CPU architectures, sounds like it's simply not worth trying to build something that old</span></div><div class="line" id="184743"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#184743" label="[18:47:43]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">adjusting source code is even more tedious when compilation speed is slow, taking my x86_64 laptop into consideration, I expect it would take at least 2 months to compile all these versions of ghc, at this point patching all ghc versions just to make it bootstrappable on different OSes and arches would take years</span></div><div class="line" id="185048"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#185048" label="[18:50:48]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like who would even try to do that just for riscv alone?</span></div><div class="line" id="185137"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#185137" label="[18:51:37]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; For riscv, the only option would be cross-compiling</span></div><div class="line" id="185206"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#185206" label="[18:52:06]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; But cross-compiling from a bootstrapped x86 version is much better than cross-compiling using an x86 binary of unknown and unverifiable provenance</span></div><div class="line" id="185637"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#185637" label="[18:56:37]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that means it's not bootstrappable on riscv, as it's impossible to build ghc by having only riscv hardware and ghc's source code</span></div><div class="line" id="185704"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#185704" label="[18:57:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; &quot;Not bootstrappable on riscv&quot; is a lesser issue than &quot;not bootstrappable, period&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="185826"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#185826" label="[18:58:26]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; In fact, even restoring the ability to go from .hc files (of any version) to a modern GHC would be a step forwared</span></div><div class="line" id="185909"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#185909" label="[18:59:09]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; If the gap from 7.4 to 7.6 can be bridged, we can have the Haskell world be rooted in 4.08's .hc files, rather than 7.6's binary distribution</span></div><div class="line" id="190200"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#190200" label="[19:02:00]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but aren't .hc files generated from .hs files, which basically makes them the same as binaries?</span></div><div class="line" id="190311"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#190311" label="[19:03:11]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; I'd consider them a step worse than mere pregenerated files, but not quite as evil as a binary of unknown provenance</span></div><div class="line" id="190623"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#190623" label="[19:06:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sounds like it's easier to have alternative compiler and work with developers of existing projects like darcs to have them buildable without ghc</span></div><div class="line" id="190738"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#190738" label="[19:07:38]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Well, good luck with that</span></div><div class="line" id="190804"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#190804" label="[19:08:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; IMO modern Haskell is _way_ too complex to plausibly write an alternative compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="190900"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#190900" label="[19:09:00]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean adjusting source code of existing projects to build under haskell98 or haskell2010</span></div><div class="line" id="190958"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#190958" label="[19:09:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Those had no integrated dependency management, which the whole modern Haskell world fundamentally depends on</span></div><div class="line" id="191026"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191026" label="[19:10:26]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you mean dependency management outside the scope of cabal?</span></div><div class="line" id="191030"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191030" label="[19:10:30]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; It's like trying to remove Maven/Gradle from the Java world, or Cargo from the Rust world</span></div><div class="line" id="191109"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191109" label="[19:11:09]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; AFAIK Cabal didn't yet exist in Haskell98, and was far more primitive in 2010</span></div><div class="line" id="191228"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191228" label="[19:12:28]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">let me guess, it's a pain to build under different compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="191234"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191234" label="[19:12:34]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to build cabal*</span></div><div class="line" id="191310"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191310" label="[19:13:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; I'm pretty sure Cabal is tightly bound to GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="191335"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191335" label="[19:13:35]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but aren't both cabal and cargo just tools like make? make is not part of C standard, but is used to build a lot of C projects</span></div><div class="line" id="191435"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191435" label="[19:14:35]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Kind of, but the problem is that these tools aren't stable, unlike Make</span></div><div class="line" id="191456"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191456" label="[19:14:56]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; you can build the latest GNU Make using a fairly old GCC, LLVM, or any other competent C compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="191512"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191512" label="[19:15:12]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; and you can use not-so-recent Make to build modern C compilers</span></div><div class="line" id="191536"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191536" label="[19:15:36]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; by contrast, you need the latest cargo to build the latest rustc, and vice versa</span></div><div class="line" id="191541"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191541" label="[19:15:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; same for cabal and GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="191907"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191907" label="[19:19:07]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://hackage.haskell.org/package/darcs-2.18.4/src/darcs.cabal">https://hackage.haskell.org/package/darcs-2.18.4/src/darcs.cabal</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="191944"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191944" label="[19:19:44]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the format itself doesn't look hard to parse under different tool</span></div><div class="line" id="192037"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192037" label="[19:20:37]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and there it also states &quot;default-language: Haskell2010&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="192229"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192229" label="[19:22:29]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">never mind, at the bottom it states which extensions it uses and that's too much</span></div><div class="line" id="193216"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#193216" label="[19:32:16]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And while make isn't part of ISO C, it is part of POSIX and has several implementations (like GNU, the various BSDs maintaining their own spin, pdpmake, illumos dmake, …)</span></div><div class="line" id="193456"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#193456" label="[19:34:56]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and those implementations are incompatible with each other as they come with their own extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="193539"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#193539" label="[19:35:39]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even syntax for &quot;if&quot; is different</span></div><div class="line" id="193551"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#193551" label="[19:35:51]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well that's also true for C and shell, most languages end up with extensions, but you can choose not to use said extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="193740"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#193740" label="[19:37:40]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, I meant the separation between standard for language, standard for libraries and standard for tools</span></div><div class="line" id="193905"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#193905" label="[19:39:05]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">haskell2010, from what I see, is standard for language and libraries, it doesn't state tools</span></div><div class="line" id="194356"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#194356" label="[19:43:56]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it doesn't look hard to create parser for .cabal files to build projects and leverage apt, guix, whatever for complicated dependency management</span></div><div class="line" id="195707"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#195707" label="[19:57:07]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; well, if haskell is so complex, maybe line by line port of modern haskell to another language could work, but that's years work porting...</span></div><div class="line" id="195748"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#195748" label="[19:57:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but hard to see any other way how to jump from old GHC to modern one (even if we can get old one, which we can't)</span></div><div class="line" id="200027"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#200027" label="[20:00:27]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the thing is even for personal programming I choose haskell simply because there is no other language that provides good looking syntax, lazy evaluation, strong type checking and IO monad, and if there is it has same bootstrap problem</span></div><div class="line" id="200124"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#200124" label="[20:01:24]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's painful to write code in haxe, lisp, erlang and others</span></div><div class="line" id="204629"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#204629" label="[20:46:29]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to those who think it would be easier to write an alternative implementation of Haskell 2010: please look at the existing alternative implementations of Haskell98.  If that's not a good enough foundation to write a 2010 implementation then you'll have a hard time closing the gap.  And if it is good enough you'd have saved all the time required to support Haskell98.</span></div><div class="line" id="204718"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#204718" label="[20:47:18]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the one big advantage of the bootstrap starting from ghc 4 is that it is known to work, because something like it has been done over the decades.</span></div><div class="line" id="204756"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#204756" label="[20:47:56]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">before ghc4 things become very difficult, because of various non-free compilers that were around at that time.</span></div><div class="line" id="204920"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#204920" label="[20:49:20]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">note also that GHC has always implemented non-standard Haskell and depends on a compiler supporting these non-standard features.</span></div><div class="line" id="205729"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205729" label="[20:57:29]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bootstrapping from ghc4 is still problematic for various os+arch combinations</span></div><div class="line" id="205856"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205856" label="[20:58:56]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">suppose netbsd on riscv32, how do you bootstrap modern ghc from ghc4?</span></div><div class="line" id="210109"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#210109" label="[21:01:09]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I won't, but this is something that could be worked around (have you looked at the GHC4 code?).  Implementing a cross-platform replacement for Haskell 2010 is just not terribly realistic, in my opinion.</span></div><div class="line" id="210248"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210248" label="[21:02:48]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">isn't hugs somewhat cross-platform haskell98?</span></div><div class="line" id="210438"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#210438" label="[21:04:38]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I have attempted to use hugs to interpret GHC before, but even with heavy modifications to the GHC sources this was not possible.  That's in no small part due to all the real world abstraction-breaking stuff in the GHC RTS.</span></div><div class="line" id="210513"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#210513" label="[21:05:13]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in the old days Hugs was able to be used as a compiler, but it required a different RTS.</span></div><div class="line" id="210952"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210952" label="[21:09:52]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">isn't hugs just interpreter?</span></div><div class="line" id="222420"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222420" label="[22:24:20]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">haskell report states that difference between haskell98 and haskell2010 is not big: added FFI, hierarchical module names and  pattern guards, but removed (n + k) patterns</span></div><div class="line" id="230025"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230025" label="[23:00:25]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GHC is not implemented using standard Haskell.</span></div><div class="line" id="230107"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230107" label="[23:01:07]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, hugs is an interpreter, but it was able to interpret the code for a Haskell compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="230125"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230125" label="[23:01:25]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which could thus be used to compile the interpreted compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="230251"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230251" label="[23:02:51]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://elephly.net/posts/2017-01-09-bootstrapping-haskell-part-1.html">https://elephly.net/posts/2017-01-09-bootstrapping-haskell-part-1.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="230300"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230300" label="[23:03:00]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you mean nhc98 on top of hugs?</span></div><div class="line" id="230311"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230311" label="[23:03:11]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's my blog post</span></div><div class="line" id="230320"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230320" label="[23:03:20]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't mean nhc98</span></div><div class="line" id="230334"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230334" label="[23:03:34]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it was just one example that I looked at</span></div><div class="line" id="230419"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230419" label="[23:04:19]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I read through a lot of old release notes, and the old GHC release notes would sometimes mention compatibility with Hugs and very old versions would come with a fork of Hugs.</span></div><div class="line" id="230515"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230515" label="[23:05:15]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is a new attempt to bootstrap haskell <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/blynn/compiler">https://github.com/blynn/compiler</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="230532"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230532" label="[23:05:32]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(there was a part 2 to the blog post, but I never bothered publishing it, because it kind of fizzled out.)</span></div><div class="line" id="230558"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230558" label="[23:05:58]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in short it's a chain of compilers compiling each other into vm code</span></div><div class="line" id="230619"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230619" label="[23:06:19]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it has been mentioned here a few years ago</span></div><div class="line" id="230655"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#230655" label="[23:06:55]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I stopped following all this a few years ago, because my amount of interest in Haskell began to match the lack of interest of the Haskell community in bootstrapping.</span></div><div class="line" id="230748"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230748" label="[23:07:48]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, I'd like to switch to smaller functional language that is bootstrappable, but I don't know of any that has 4 basic things at the same time</span></div><div class="line" id="232301"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232301" label="[23:23:01]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, it's surprising all implementations are abandoned</span></div><div class="line" id="233823"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233823" label="[23:38:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">small vm seems to be promising approach as it reduces the amount of code necessary to support different oses and arches, at least until ffi is hit</span></div><div class="line" id="234027"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#234027" label="[23:40:27]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">os+arch combinations are a non-issue for me when considering higher level bootstraps, as QEMU and cross-compilation exists ......</span></div><div class="line" id="234318"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#234318" label="[23:43:18]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: there is also a pijul crate on cargo, which is hopefully the same as the vcs data :)</span></div><div class="line" id="234420"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#234420" label="[23:44:20]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">blynn's compiler still seems to be very far off anywhere near haskell compilation unfortunately</span></div><div class="line" id="234421"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#234421" label="[23:44:21]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mid-kid: crates are often different to the VCS, for eg the rand crate contains Rust code generated by a Python script, which is not in the crate but only in the VCS</span></div><div class="line" id="234514"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234514" label="[23:45:14]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pabs3: obviously, as freebsd downloads pijul.crate</span></div><div class="line" id="234702"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234702" label="[23:47:02]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">advantage of blynn's compiler is writing haskell in a tiny subset of haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="235411"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#235411" label="[23:54:11]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://radicle.xyz/">https://radicle.xyz/</a>  does the same as pijul</span></div><br /></div></body></html>